Firmware bug, patched for the host build only — fix it upstream and delete this file.

CanManager_new() receives read_messageLimit[] / write_messageLimit[] but
never stores them in the object, so me->read_messageLimit and
me->write_messageLimit keep whatever malloc handed back. Both are used as
the size of stack arrays (IO_CAN_DATA_FRAME canMessages[...]) in
CanManager_read and canOutput_sendDebugMessage0, and as the FIFO read
size. On the host the garbage size corrupts the stack on the first main
loop cycle; on the target it depends on what is in RAM at boot.

--- a/canManager.c
+++ b/canManager.c
@@ -57,6 +57,8 @@
     me->sendDelayus = defaultSendDelayus;
 
     for (int i=0; i < 2; ++ i){
+        me->read_messageLimit[i] = read_messageLimit[i];
+        me->write_messageLimit[i] = write_messageLimit[i];
         //Activate the CAN channels --------------------------------------------------
         me->ioErr_Init[i] = IO_CAN_Init(IO_CAN_CHANNEL_0+i, busSpeed[i], 0, 0, 0);
 
